es and local councils. Left out because there is no IMF exchange rate or dollar GDP to turn them into dollars safely: West Bank and Gaza. Years left out because the dollar amount fails the check against the IMF's own dollar GDP (usually a currency unit that differs between two IMF tables, or a redenomination): Argentina 1950-1962; Bulgaria 1997-2024; Bolivia 1970-1979, 1981-1985; Cameroon 1994; Democratic Republic of the Congo 1996, 1998, 2000-2001, 2024; Ecuador 1990-1999; Egypt 1990-1991; Ethiopia 1992; Georgia 1995; Ghana 1962-1979; Guatemala 1986; Guyana 1990; Honduras 1990-1999; Iran 1985-1987, 1991-1992, 2012, 2018-2023; Kazakhstan 1993; Kuwait 1991; Laos 1976-1979; Lebanon 2020-2023; Liberia 1973-1983, 2000-2024; Mali 1970-1981, 1989; Myanmar 1970-1980, 1989-1994, 1998-2012; Mauritania 2004; Nigeria 2011-2024; Sudan 2012, 2014-2020; Sierra Leone 1970-2024; El Salvador 1991-2000; Serbia 2000; South Sudan 2017; S\u00e3o Tom\u00e9 and Pr\u00edncipe 1991; Suriname 1990-1993, 2020; Syria 1989-2010; Turkmenistan 1997-2001; Tanzania 1986-1987; Uganda 1970-1986; Uruguay 1970-1979; Venezuela 1998-2017; Yemen 1990-2023; Zambia 1970-1979; Zimbabwe 1964-2003, 2005-2008, 2019-2024. It counts general government debt, or central government where that is all there is. Euro members' GDP is stated in euros for every year, so before a country joined the euro its national rate is divided by the fixed conversion rate. Source: International Monetary Fund."}